\chapter{MIR-to-C11 and the backend registry}
\label{ch:82}
\index{MIR-to-C11 and the backend registry}

The \OnxCodeTerm{mir-c11} adapter consumes verified MIR and emits C11 using its explicit block, value and checked-operation semantics. The core tool also registers MIR text, CFG and evaluator adapters. SDK-enabled drivers can dispatch trusted built-ins through the registry and artifact sink. With the extension SDK disabled, equivalent core modes use the canonical compiler routines. Optional Yellowstone integration generates the registry map at build time.

\section{Compare execution through two paths}
Choose a scalar fixture that both direct C11 and MIR support. Build one native binary from each and compare outputs and failure behavior for the same edge inputs. If behavior differs, evaluate MIR independently to determine whether the problem appears before or during MIR C emission. Compare semantics before comparing formatting or symbol names. A built-in adapter gets private host-controlled representations. Discoverable external plugins use the separately versioned SDK channels described in \cref{ch:112}.

\section{Distinguish wire errors from program errors}
Consider two requests for C text from the same scalar MIR function.
Inside the compiler the trusted adapter can synchronously borrow a module
that has already been checked. In an ordinary SDK invocation it instead
receives canonical bytes, deserializes the module, and verifies what was
decoded. Both call the same MIR C emitter. For a small valid input, compare
the entire C translation units: identical output here tests the adapter's
transport and dispatch, rather than equivalence between two independently
implemented code generators. Neither request has yet compiled the C.

Now give the byte route an incomplete serialization. A syntax error points
into the MIR buffer because the decoder could not reconstruct a module.
Contrast a syntactically complete serialization carrying a bad operand
and a valid recorded source location. A verifier diagnostic about that
operand can refer to the decoded program's filename and coordinates.
Looking up both errors at the same line in the original On1x source would
confuse the carrier with the program it describes.

Finally imagine that verification and emission succeed but the output sink
has insufficient room. The adapter must propagate the limit and return
no successful artifact; the host decides publication. Conversely, a
large in-memory module can fit the private route while exceeding the
wire reader's independent byte limit. Such a discrepancy calls for
measuring the encoded request before investigating the semantics of the
emitted C. The registry's declaration of a MIR input phase does not make
every transport equally capacious.

